<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bamberg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bamberg is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-franconia">Upper Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, on the Regnitz near the Main. Emperor Henry II founded its bishopric in 1007, and the cathedral holds his tomb and the statue of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg-horseman">Bamberg Horseman</a>. The prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> ruled the city until 1802, and its old town is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. In 1919 the Bavarian government took refuge there and adopted the Bamberg Constitution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bamberg.jpg" alt="Bamberg" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reinhard Kirchner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kloster_Michaelsberg_Parkseite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Imperial Recess of 1803 had already given Bavaria the Franconian prince-bishoprics of <b>Bamberg</b> and Würzburg (the latter given up again in 1805), other church lands and imperial cities, and the Peace of Pressburg in 1805 added Tyrol, Vorarlberg and further Swabian territory together with full sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry III, king of Germany since 1039, was crowned Holy Roman Emperor by Pope Clement II, the former bishop of <b>Bamberg</b>, whom Henry had made pope after the Synod of Sutri removed three rival claimants.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-25">25 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the murder of Bavarian premier Kurt Eisner in February 1919, the elected government of Johannes Hoffmann fled Munich to <b>Bamberg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bavarian-soviet-republic">Bavarian Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Their family is among the oldest noble houses in Franconia, and they held lands between Würzburg and <b>Bamberg</b>, often as vassals of the Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg, while claiming imperial immediacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-castell">County of Castell</a></li><li><blockquote>Otto did not appoint a new duke; Franconia passed directly to the crown and fragmented into bishoprics such as Würzburg and <b>Bamberg</b> and later secular lordships.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-franconia">Duchy of Franconia</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Bamberg</b> constitution of 1919 established Bavaria as a parliamentary free state.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-bavaria">Free State of Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
